# {{.DisplayName}}

This project uses {{if eq .UI "vue"}}Vue{{else}}React{{end}} for its UI and Go for its RPC backend.

## Build the frontend

With Node.js 22.12+ or a supported newer LTS, npm, Go, and the Bridra CLI on your PATH:

```sh
cd frontend
npm ci
npm run build
cd ..{{if hasPlatform "web"}}
bridra build web{{end}}
```

{{if hasPlatform "web"}}The last command packages the existing `frontend/dist/` under `build/bridra/web/` with a SHA-256 manifest. Keep the dist and manifest together.{{else}}The compiled frontend is in `frontend/dist/`.{{end}}
{{if or (hasPlatform "macos") (hasPlatform "windows") (hasPlatform "linux")}}
## Build the desktop application

On the matching OS, run one of the following commands from the project root.
{{if hasPlatform "web"}}Copy the complete `build/bridra/web/<sha256>/` snapshot here first. Replace
`<sha256>` with the digest printed by `bridra build web`.{{else}}The CLI snapshots
the compiled frontend and includes its SHA-256 in the desktop build output.{{end}}

```text
{{if hasPlatform "macos"}}bridra build macos{{if hasPlatform "web"}} --web-dist-sha256 <sha256>{{end}}
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "windows"}}bridra build windows{{if hasPlatform "web"}} --web-dist-sha256 <sha256>{{end}}
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "linux"}}bridra build linux{{if hasPlatform "web"}} --web-dist-sha256 <sha256>{{end}}
{{end}}```

{{if hasPlatform "macos"}}macOS needs the Xcode command-line tools. Open the resulting `.app`.
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "windows"}}Windows needs an MSVC Native Tools prompt, Windows SDK, and WebView2 SDK.
Set `BRIDRA_WEBVIEW2_SDK` to the extracted SDK. The app uses the installed Edge WebView2 Runtime.
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "linux"}}Linux needs a C compiler, pkg-config and development packages for GTK 4, WebKitGTK 6.0 and libsoup 3.
{{end}}Keep all files in the printed artifact together. The native window uses the
packaged Go backend and a private per-launch session.
{{end}}

For local UI development run `npx vite` from `frontend/`. To exercise authenticated operations in a browser, serve the UI and the RPC endpoint on the same origin as described below.

## Connect the backend

From `backend/`, start `go run ./cmd/server --listen 127.0.0.1:8081` with a private `BRIDRA_BACKEND_TOKEN` supplied through the server environment. For a website, serve the packaged dist at the origin root. Route `POST /rpc` through your server-side authentication layer to the Go server. Validate the user's application session, authorize the operation, and check the request origin/CSRF policy before attaching the backend bearer. Never put this token in the frontend bundle or a Vite environment variable. Requests without a valid session should receive HTTP 401.

The frontend uses same-origin cookies and calls relative `/rpc`. The starter includes health and greeting examples, and the generated TypeScript client supports unary RPC. Use `bridra generate` from the project root after editing `schema/bridra.json`. Use `bridra generate --check` to verify generated files.

## Try the protected website locally

The optional Caddy 2 configuration at the project root serves the built website
on loopback. It uses the browser's built-in password prompt for a local preview.
For a deployed application, use your own user sessions and authorization rules.

After building the website, run from the project root with Caddy and OpenSSL
available. The first command prompts for a preview password. Sign in as `dev`
with that password when opening `http://127.0.0.1:8090/`.

```sh
(
  set -eu
  BRIDRA_PREVIEW_HASH=$(caddy hash-password)
  BRIDRA_BACKEND_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
  export BRIDRA_PREVIEW_HASH BRIDRA_BACKEND_TOKEN
  mkdir -p build
  (cd backend && go build -o ../build/preview-backend ./cmd/server)
  ./build/preview-backend --listen 127.0.0.1:8081 &
  backend_pid=$!
  trap 'kill "$backend_pid" 2>/dev/null || true; wait "$backend_pid" 2>/dev/null || true' EXIT
  caddy run --config Caddyfile
)
```

Use Ctrl+C to stop the preview. The configuration serves `frontend/dist` by
default.{{if hasPlatform "web"}} Set `BRIDRA_PREVIEW_DIST` to the artifact path printed by
`bridra build web` to open a specific packaged version.{{end}}
